      <title>Can this experimental orchard save the UK’s cider industry?</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/17/farming-apple-growers-climate-breakdown-thatchers-cider-wildflowers-soil-water-insects-aoe</link>
      <description>Farmers are utilising science and nature to help orchards adapt and thrive in the face of shifting temperatures, rainfall and pests On Myrtle Farm in Somerset, there are thousands of apple trees laid out in rows of 100. Over the past year, each has endured the same wet winter and long summer drought . Now, the different ways each tree responded to these challenges are being analysed in trials that could shape the future of apple growing – and the taste of cider – for decades to come. Myrtle…</description>
